      en: { title: 'The Explorer the Amazon Swallowed', setup: "The British explorer Percy Fawcett believed the Amazon hid the ruins of an advanced civilization he called the city of 'Z', and that belief became the single purpose of his life.", event: 'As David Grann recounts, Fawcett set out on his final expedition in 1925 with his son and his son’s friend and was never heard from again; dozens who went to search for him also died or vanished.', turn: 'For a long time Fawcett was dismissed as an obsessed dreamer, yet in recent years archaeologists have found evidence that complex Amazonian societies with road networks, agriculture, and large settlements truly existed.', outcome: 'For Grann, Fawcett was both right and ruined: the civilization he sought was largely real, but his obsession dragged him and his followers into catastrophe.', lesson: 'A strong intuition can be correct, yet without the discipline to test it, that same intuition can lead someone off a cliff.', question: 'Which strong belief are you still chasing without testing whether it is true?', punch: 'The lost civilization Fawcett sought was real, but his obsession with it swallowed him beyond return.', contrast: 'Right intuition, ruinous obsession' },
